# Amazon EKS Zero to Hero (2026 Edition)

# Chapter 9 – EKS Storage Deep Dive

## Learning Objectives

After completing this chapter, you will understand:

* Kubernetes storage architecture
* Why containers need persistent storage
* Volumes in Kubernetes
* Persistent Volumes (PV)
* Persistent Volume Claims (PVC)
* Storage Classes
* Dynamic provisioning
* AWS EBS CSI Driver
* AWS EFS CSI Driver
* Stateful applications on EKS
* Database storage patterns
* Backup and recovery strategies

Storage is critical because containers are temporary, but application data must survive container restarts, Pod failures, and node replacements.

---

# 1. Why Kubernetes Storage?

Containers are temporary.

Example:

```text
Container

↓

Application Data

↓

Container Deleted

↓

Data Lost
```

Problems:

* Pod restarts
* Node failures
* Application upgrades
* Scaling events

Solution:

```text
Container

↓

Persistent Storage

↓

Data Survives
```

---

# 2. Storage Architecture in Kubernetes

Kubernetes separates:

1. Application
2. Storage request
3. Physical storage

Architecture:

```text
Application Pod

        |

        |

Persistent Volume Claim (PVC)

        |

        |

Persistent Volume (PV)

        |

        |

Cloud Storage

(EBS / EFS)
```

---

# 3. Kubernetes Storage Objects

Main storage objects:

| Object                        | Purpose                     |
| ----------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Volume                        | Storage attached to Pod     |
| Persistent Volume (PV)        | Actual storage resource     |
| Persistent Volume Claim (PVC) | Request for storage         |
| StorageClass                  | Creates storage dynamically |

---

# 4. Kubernetes Volume

A Volume provides storage to containers.

Example:

```text
Pod

|

Container

|

Volume
```

---

Types:

* emptyDir
* hostPath
* ConfigMap volume
* Secret volume
* Persistent Volume

---

# 5. emptyDir Volume

Temporary storage.

Created when Pod starts.

Example:

```yaml
volumes:

- name: temporary-storage

  emptyDir: {}
```

Lifecycle:

```text
Pod Created

↓

Volume Created

↓

Pod Deleted

↓

Volume Deleted
```

---

Use cases:

* Cache
* Temporary files
* Sharing data between containers

---

# 6. hostPath Volume

Mounts storage from the node.

Example:

```text
Worker Node

/home/data

      |

      |

Pod
```

Used mainly for:

* Testing
* Node-level applications

Not recommended for production workloads.

---

# 7. Persistent Volume (PV)

A Persistent Volume represents actual storage.

Example:

```text
AWS EBS Volume

        |

        |

Persistent Volume

        |

        |

Pod
```

A PV exists independently from Pods.

---

Example PV:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1

kind: PersistentVolume

metadata:

  name: database-pv


spec:

  capacity:

    storage: 20Gi
```

---

# 8. Persistent Volume Claim (PVC)

PVC is a storage request from an application.

Example:

Application says:

"I need 20GB storage."

PVC:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1

kind: PersistentVolumeClaim

metadata:

  name: mysql-pvc


spec:

  resources:

    requests:

      storage: 20Gi
```

---

Flow:

```text
Developer

↓

PVC Request

↓

PV Matching

↓

Storage Attached

↓

Pod Uses Storage
```

---

# 9. PV Access Modes

Access modes define how storage can be mounted.

---

## ReadWriteOnce (RWO)

One node can mount storage.

Example:

```text
Node 1

 |

 EBS Volume
```

Common for:

* Databases
* MySQL
* PostgreSQL

---

## ReadOnlyMany (ROX)

Multiple nodes can read.

---

## ReadWriteMany (RWX)

Multiple nodes can read/write.

Example:

```text
Pod 1

 |

 |

EFS

 |

 |

Pod 2
```

Common for:

* Shared files
* Content storage

---

# 10. StorageClass

StorageClass creates storage automatically.

Without StorageClass:

```text
Administrator

↓

Create PV manually
```

With StorageClass:

```text
Developer

↓

PVC

↓

StorageClass

↓

Automatically creates PV

↓

Cloud Storage
```

---

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1

kind: StorageClass

metadata:

 name: gp3


provisioner:

 ebs.csi.aws.com
```

---

# 11. Dynamic Provisioning

Dynamic provisioning automatically creates storage.

Example:

```text
Application

↓

PVC

↓

StorageClass

↓

AWS EBS

↓

Volume Created Automatically
```

Benefits:

* No manual storage creation
* Faster deployments
* Easier scaling

---

# 12. AWS Storage Options for EKS

Main AWS storage services:

## Amazon EBS

Block storage

## Amazon EFS

Shared file storage

---

# 13. Amazon EBS Overview

Amazon Elastic Block Store provides block storage.

Architecture:

```text
EC2 Worker Node

        |

        |

      EBS Volume

        |

        |

       Pod
```

---

Characteristics:

* High performance
* Low latency
* Single node attachment

---

Best for:

* Databases
* Stateful applications
* High-performance workloads

---

# 14. EBS CSI Driver

Kubernetes communicates with EBS through CSI.

CSI:

**Container Storage Interface**

Architecture:

```text
Kubernetes

↓

EBS CSI Driver

↓

Amazon EBS

↓

Volume
```

---

Install check:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
```

Look for:

```text
ebs-csi-controller
```

---

# 15. EBS StorageClass Example

```yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1

kind: StorageClass

metadata:

 name: gp3


provisioner:

 ebs.csi.aws.com


parameters:

 type: gp3
```

---

# 16. EBS Limitations

EBS:

* Attached to one Availability Zone
* Usually one node attachment
* Not shared filesystem

Example:

```text
AZ-A

Node 1

 |

EBS Volume
```

Cannot easily attach to:

```text
AZ-B

Node 2
```

---

# 17. Amazon EFS Overview

Amazon Elastic File System provides shared storage.

Architecture:

```text
             EFS


        /           \


     Pod 1          Pod 2
```

---

Characteristics:

* Multi-node access
* Shared filesystem
* Automatic scaling

---

Best for:

* Shared files
* Web applications
* Content management systems

---

# 18. EFS CSI Driver

Architecture:

```text
Kubernetes

↓

EFS CSI Driver

↓

Amazon EFS

↓

Pods
```

---

# 19. EBS vs EFS

| Feature     | EBS           | EFS            |
| ----------- | ------------- | -------------- |
| Type        | Block Storage | File Storage   |
| Access      | Single node   | Multiple nodes |
| Performance | High          | Shared         |
| Use Case    | Database      | Shared files   |
| Multi-AZ    | No            | Yes            |

---

# 20. Stateful Applications in EKS

Stateless:

Example:

```text
Frontend

API Server

Web Server
```

Data stored elsewhere.

---

Stateful:

Example:

```text
Database

Message Queue

Storage System
```

Need:

* Stable identity
* Persistent storage
* Ordered deployment

Use:

* StatefulSet
* Persistent Volumes

---

# 21. StatefulSet with Storage

Architecture:

```text
StatefulSet

      |

      |

Pod mysql-0

      |

      |

PVC mysql-0

      |

      |

EBS Volume
```

If Pod restarts:

```text
Old Pod

mysql-0


New Pod

mysql-0


Same Storage
```

---

# 22. Database Deployment Pattern

Recommended production approach:

Instead of:

```text
Database inside Kubernetes
```

Often:

```text
Application Pods

        |

        |

Amazon RDS Database
```

AWS managed databases:

* Automated backups
* Maintenance
* High availability

---

Use Kubernetes databases when:

* Need portability
* Require custom database setup
* Have Kubernetes expertise

